Hi there!
I don't know if I understand you correctly. But I did some tests here. I created this window with a sprite inside of it:
JavaScript:
class myWin extends Window_Base{
    initialize(rect){
        super.initialize(rect)
        const bitmap = ImageManager.loadPicture("ilia")
        bitmap.addLoadListener(() => {
            this.mySpr = new Sprite(bitmap)
            this.mySpr.x = (this.width-bitmap.width)/2
            this.mySpr.y = (this.height-bitmap.height)/2
            this.addChild(this.mySpr)
        })

    }
}
Then I made some tests on the console, making the window the size of my game screen/resolution:
On the first setFrame, I put X and Y to 10. And when I call the function, I realize that it seems that the Sprite moved -10x and -10y. To fix this, I adjusted the sprite position to +10x and +10y.
Now, for me to give the impression that I'm cutting off the right and bottom of the sprite, I did the second call of the setFrame, but this time changing the width and height of the frame.
My conclusion with this is, whathever you set the frame of the sprite and want to give the impression that he is on the same place, just adjust the sprite position X and Y according to the number you put on the setFrame.
Now, if I did misunderstood what you mean, this is my second attempt!! haha!
I would do the .blt only after the bitmap is loaded, like
@utunnels
said.
If perhaps what you want is that the sprite only shows the "cutted" version of your bitmap, instead of putting the whole bitmap inside the sprite and use the .blt later, do it before, and pass into the sprite a new bitmap with the result of the .blt.
Example:
JavaScript:
class myWin extends Window_Base{
    initialize(rect){
        super.initialize(rect)
        const faceSetImage = ImageManager.loadFace("Monster")
        faceSetImage.addLoadListener(() => {
            const face = new Bitmap(144,144) // Size of a single face inside the faceSet Image
            const sx = 144*2
            const sy = 0
            const sw = 144
            const sh = 144
            const dx = 0
            const dy = 0
            face.blt(faceSetImage, sx, sy, sw, sh, dx, dy)
            this.mySpr = new Sprite(face)
            this.mySpr.x = (this.width-face.width)/2
            this.mySpr.y = (this.height-face.height)/2
            this.addChild(this.mySpr)

        })
    }
}
Hope it helps!

